ying it out loud—watching his fake charm crack, his too-wide mouth twist. But I didn’t. I never did.</p><p>Instead I tilted my head, forcing the lie softer.</p><p>He chuckled, low and pleased, stepping half a pace closer.</p><p>The cologne hit me again—thick, musky, expensive in the worst way.</p><p>“There we go. Much better.” His gaze dropped deliberately to my mouth, then lower, lingering on the neckline of my dress like he was appraising a purchase.</p><p>“You’ve grown up beautifully, Alina. Truly. Your father must be proud.”</p><p>My father’s proud of the price tag you might bring, not me.</p><p>I swallowed the bile rising in my throat.</p><p>“Thank you,” I managed, voice small and polite—the perfect doll.</p><p>His hand lifted, fingers brushing the bare skin just above my elbow—light, but deliberate.</p><p>The same touch he’d used when I was fifteen, sixteen, seventeen. Always “accidental.”</p><p>Always when no one was looking close enough.</p><p>“I was just telling Arthur how much I’ve been looking forward to tonight,” he continued.</p><p>“A young woman like you… so full of potential.” The word came out low, syrupy, the way men like him say “potential” when what they really mean is available.</p><p>He leaned in closer, breath carrying the faint metallic tang of expensive scotch and older-man cologne that had probably gone out of fashion before I was born. His voice dropped to that intimate register sleazy men reserve for propositions disguised as compliments.</p><p>“You know, sweetheart, a girl with your… assets… could go far in our world. Very far. I’d be more than happy to show you the ropes. Personally.”</p><p>The implication hung there, heavy and sickening.</p><p>My stomach twisted so hard I thought I might be sick right on his polished shoes.</p><p>“That sounds… interesting,” I lied, the words brittle. I eased my arm back, trying to break the touch without making it obvious.</p><p>His fingers tightened for a heartbeat—possessive—before releasing with a reluctant slide.</p><p>“Don’t be shy, darling. We’re practically family already.” Another chuckle, like we were sharing a private joke.</p><p>“Tell me, what does a beautiful girl like you want? I can give anything you. Name it.”</p><p>I want you to spontaneously combust.</p><p>I want my father to develop a sudden allergy to money and shut the hell up.</p><p>I want a portal to open under my feet and drop me into literally any other zip code on planet Earth. Bonus points if it’s one with Wi-Fi and zero creepy old men.</p><p>“No need…” I said instead, smile twitching like it was having a small seizure.</p><p>“Liar,” he teased, voice dropping intimate, conspiratorial.</p><p>“I can always tell when a woman’s holding back what she really wants.”</p><p>His eyes gleamed with something dark and patient—like a man who knew he’d get what he wanted eventually.</p><p>“If you’ll excuse me—” I started again, firmer this time, already turning.</p><p>“Yeah. I really need to use the loo,” I said, injecting maximum urgency into my voice—</p><p>“Like, emergency levels. It’s been a long evening of tiny canapés and cocktails. My insides are staging a revolt. If I don’t get to porcelain throne in the next ninety seconds, we’re all going to be reading about this in the society pages tomorrow under the headline ‘Santini Heiress Causes International Incident in Restroom.’”</p><p>I nodded—jerky, mechanical—and walked away, legs unsteady, heels clicking too loud in my ears.</p><p>Behind me, I heard my father call my name again, irritation threading through it.</p><p>His hand clamped around my wrist before I made it ten steps—hard, unyielding.</p><p>He yanked me sideways, pulling me into a shadowed alcove off the main ballroom, away from the glittering lights and the curious glances.</p><p>The moment we were out of sight, he spun me to face him, fingers digging into my arm like he was trying to leave marks.</p><p>“What the hell was that?” he hissed, voice pitched low and venomous.</p>"}]
