 Not even a hint of concern. I could be dying and he'd still prioritize his fucking pack.</p><p>I barely made it to the hospital. The emergency room doctor's words hammered through my skull: \\"You were pregnant. Early miscarriage. Did you know?\\"</p><p>I hadn't known. Six weeks along, they said. Drake's child. Lost. A baby. I was carrying a baby. And now it's gone, before I even knew it existed.</p><p>\\"Is your mate coming?\\" the nurse asked.</p><p>\\"I don't have a mate,\\" I whispered. Just a contract. Just business. Just a decade of my life signed away to a man who sees me as a convenient hole to fuck.</p><p>The next day, for the first time in ten years, I didn't show up at Stone Industries. I spent the day in a hospital bed, staring at the ceiling, wondering how I'd ended up here—an Omega trapped in a decade-long contract with an Alpha who saw me as nothing but propert. How did I let this happen?</p><p>My mind drifted back to that night ten years ago. The Eclipse Club—an exclusive establishment where powerful werewolves conducted business away from prying eyes. I'd been desperate, my mother's silver poisoning treatment costs mounting daily. No insurance would cover an Omega with her condition.</p><p>I remembered the weight of the tray in my hands as I served drinks, how Drake's eyes had locked onto me from across the room. He'd summoned me to his table, those golden eyes assessing me like a commodity.</p><p>\\"An unmated Omega,\\" he'd said, nostrils flaring. \\"Working here of all places.\\"</p><p>\\"I need the money,\\" I'd answered, more boldly than an Omega should address an Alpha.</p><p>That night, after my shift, he'd been waiting in his black Bentley. \\"I have a proposition for you.\\"</p><p>The terms had been clear: a ten-year contract. I would be his assistant publicly, his temporary mate privately. The salary would more than cover my mother's treatments. In exchange, I belonged to him—exclusively.</p><p>\\"Sign here,\\" he'd said, sliding the dual